  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Consider the quadratic $y=ax^2+bx+c$. Describe the path of the vertex of this parabola as $b$ is varied (but $a$ and $c$ are held fixed).",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The vertex traces the parabola $y=-ax^2+c$, with vertex $(0,c)$ and axis of symmetry $x=0$.\n\nEliminate $b$ from the vertex coordinates $\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a},c-\\frac{b^2}{4a}\\right)$.",
      "worked": "Completing the square gives\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=a\\left(x^2+\\frac{b}{a}x\\right)+c\\\\y&=a\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)^2+c-\\frac{b^2}{4a}.\\end{align*}$$\nWrite the vertex as $(h,k)$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}h&=-\\frac{b}{2a}\\\\b&=-2ah\\\\k&=c-\\frac{b^2}{4a}\\\\k&=c-\\frac{(-2ah)^2}{4a}\\\\k&=c-ah^2.\\end{align*}$$\nAs $b$ varies, $h$ takes every real value. The vertex therefore traces $y=-ax^2+c$, a parabola with vertex $(0,c)$, opening downwards if $a>0$ and upwards if $a<0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
